> **W2** The finding that LLMs fail at efficiency analysis is fantastic, but the paper just reports this finding and moves on. This is precisely the point where the work should have dug in. Why do they fail?Is it because they can't distinguish between O(n^2) and O(n \log n)? Is it a failure in comparative reasoning? The paper identifies a critical problem but offers no diagnosis. This insight is strong, but as presented, it's not quite enough to carry the paper on its own.
> 

Based on our further analysis, Big-O differences are not the main source of errors. Among the 54 cases where one program TLEs and the other ACs—indicating a clear gap in asymptotic complexity—LLMs perform very well (gpt-5-cot even reaches 100% accuracy). This suggests that failures mostly arise when two solutions share the same Big-O complexity but differ in constant factors, language-level optimizations, or sensitivity to input size.

